// Saying what can fail, and raising a failure.
//
// `fails` lists exactly the failures that can leave a callable, before
// its `->`. A missing one is an error, and so is one that can never
// happen, so the list is always true. `reject` raises a failure: it names
// a declared failure and gives every client and internal field.

use sales/types { CakeCount, Stock }
use sales/failures { CakeSoldOut }

/// Sets `asked` cakes aside from the `left` on the shelf.
action reserve_cakes(left: Stock, asked: CakeCount) fails CakeSoldOut -> CakeCount {
    // A call that can fail must say so where it is made. `attempt` passes
    // the call's failures on to this action's caller, so `CakeSoldOut` is
    // in this action's `fails` list too. `attempt` always starts a
    // statement, so every place a callable can stop early is at the start
    // of a line.
    var reserved: CakeCount = attempt check_shelf(left, asked)
    return reserved
}

/// `asked`, if the shelf has that many left. Functions can fail too:
/// they declare `fails` and `reject` exactly as actions do.
function check_shelf(left: Stock, asked: CakeCount) fails CakeSoldOut -> CakeCount {
    // `Stock(asked)` moves the count into `Stock` so the two can be
    // compared: ordering needs the same type on both sides. It is an
    // honest conversion: cakes asked for and cakes on the shelf are both
    // counts of the same cakes, so comparing them means something. (The
    // compiler would convert a weight into a price just as readily if you
    // wrote it, which is why it never converts on its own.) Every
    // `CakeCount` (1 to 24) is a valid `Stock` (0 to 500), so the compiler
    // proves this can't fail.
    if (Stock(asked) > left) {
        reject CakeSoldOut {
            left: left
            asked: asked
        }
    }
    return asked
}
